INSERT INTO `posts` VALUES (2,'Vietnam’s Diverse Journey: From Ancient Roots to Coastal Wonders','vietnam-s-diverse-journey-from-ancient-roots-to-coastal-wonders',NULL,'Vietnam captivates global travelers with its rich tapestry of heritage, breathtaking landscapes, and vibrant cultures spanning from north to south.\r\n\r\nFor history enthusiasts, the northern midlands offer a deep spiritual connection at Phu Tho, the legendary birthplace of the nation. Moving toward the coast, Quang Ninh stuns visitors with Ha Long Bay’s emerald waters and limestone islets. Nearby, Ninh Binh displays spectacular inland karst peaks, while Lao Cai’s Sapa features misty mountains and cascading rice terraces.\r\n\r\nCentral Vietnam reveals timeless heritage and coastal charm. Quang Nam hosts the lantern-lit streets of Hoi An Ancient Town, while Thua Thien Hue preserves imperial palaces along the Perfume River. Modern Da Nang boasts sunny beaches and the iconic Golden Bridge, contrastingly complemented by Quang Binh’s world-renowned subterranean caves in Phong Nha.\r\n\r\nIn the south, Ho Chi Minh City energizes visitors with modern skyscrapers and historical landmarks. Further south, Can Tho offers authentic river life at floating markets, Lam Dong\'s Da Lat provides cool highland romanticism, and Kien Giang’s Phu Quoc Island delivers a pristine tropical beach paradise.\r\n\r\nTogether, these unique destinations create an unforgettable Vietnamese journey filled with rich traditions, scenic wonders, and warm hospitality.',NULL,1,NULL,NULL,'2026-10-18 09:18:21','2026-11-02 04:59:50'),(3,'A locals guide to the 11 best Christmas markets in France.','a-locals-guide-to-the-11-best-christmas-markets-in-france',1,'The Christmas Markets in France bring a fun festive atmosphere to France. From the renowned Strasbourg Christmas market to other less publicized but equally enchanting markets, embark with me on a discovery of the most beautiful Christmas Markets in France.\n\nThe 11 most beautiful Christmas markets in France.\n\nThese markets are the vibrant heart of the festive season. And although France is home to more than 300 markets, I have carefully chosen the best 11 for you to choose from.\n\n1. Paris: The unmissable Christmas markets.\n\nAddress: Tuileries Gardens, 75001 Paris, France\n\nEvery year, Paris is transformed into a dazzling festive setting, making its Christmas markets major attractions for locals and tourists.\n\nThe streets come alive, offering a range of activities and events. One of the highlights is the Paris Notre-Dame Christmas market. Nestled on the outskirts of the famous cathedral and the picturesque Viviani Square, this market offers a magical spectacle, enhanced by the oldest tree in Paris.\n\nThe La Défense Christmas Village stands out as the largest Christmas market in Île-de-France. With more than 300 exhibitors, this true festive paradise amazes with its varied activities, thousands of light decorations, and gourmet stands.\n\nThen for more festive cheer, head to the Place de la Concorde and the Tuileries Gardens, where “The Magic of Christmas” operates. This traditional Christmas market offers French culinary delights and artisanal creations.\n\n2. Strasbourg: A magical Christmas market experience.\n\nAddress: Cathedral Square & Place Broglie, Strasbourg, France\n\nStrasbourg, often nicknamed the capital of Christmas hosts one of the most majestic Christmas markets in France. With 300 chalets, a monumental fir tree, and a myriad of shows illuminated by countless sparkling garlands, the experience is magical.\n\nPlanning your visit requires a little organization because the Strasbourg market is divided into 3 distinct zones. Magical Christmas is the traditional market, where Places Kléber, Broglie, Petite France, Place Gutenberg, and Place de la Cathedral come alive and transform into a magical world.\n\nNext door, Noël Secret invites you to a more intimate experience in the heart of Strasbourg. There are craft workshops at the Gayot market square, as well as daily stories and shows at the Mathias Mérian square for the pleasure of young and old. Finally, The OFF Market highlights the social and solidarity economy.\n\nOn the list of must-sees is the giant fir tree on Place Kléber, the cathedral’s nativity scene, the legendary “Christkindlmärik” arch on Place Broglie, or the luminescent blue tree on Place Gutenberg.\n\n3. Reims: The Champagne Christmas market.\n\nAddress: Promenades Jean-Louis Schneiter, 51100 Reims, France\n\nEnchanting and sparkling, the Reims Christmas market stands out as an essential stopover during the end-of-year festivities in France. With its prestigious location on the cathedral square, this market is a haven of light where dozens of chalets are erected, offering a selection of food, artisanal artifacts, and stands dedicated to mulled wine tasting.\n\nThe Palais de Tau houses the artisans’ village, as well as an ice rink for children, a small train, and a traditional crèche. The Children’s Kingdom amazes with its interactive machines and visits from Santa Claus.\n\nFor a unique experience, take to the skies on the Ferris wheel located on Place d’Erlon. And for a modern touch, the balls of light scattered throughout the city center will add a touch of magic to your walk.\n\nEvery weekend, the market comes alive with the sound of concerts and other cultural events. Every day, a breathtaking sound and light show enhances this festive scene. A moment not to be missed: the lighting of the cathedral, revealing every architectural detail of this Gothic jewel.\n\n4. Rennes: The magic of Breton Christmas.\n\nAddress: Pl. du Colombier, 35000 Rennes, France\n\nAlsace is often first on everyone’s mind when it comes to French Christmas Markets. But Brittany, with Rennes in the lead, demonstrates every year that it also knows how to immerse its visitors in the magic of Christmas.\n\nIn the lively heart of Rennes, multiple markets are set up. Place du Colombier is the main gathering point where, for a month, activities, shows, and festive stands captivate passers-by.\n\nChildren can indulge in the joys of the traditional carousel installed especially for the occasion. And, within the Colombia shopping center, you will be charmed by a selection of chalets offering mulled wine, unique gift ideas, and local Breton products.\n\nHowever, the most famous Christmas market in Rennes takes place on the Mail François Mitterand. Formerly located on Parliament Square, this market offers a magical landscape with nearly forty chalets, a myriad of light decorations, an illuminated Ferris wheel, and a traditional wooden horse carousel that will delight young and old alike.\n\n5. Bordeaux: Tradition and discoveries at the Christmas market.\n\nAddress: All. de Tourny, 33000 Bordeaux, France\n\nEvery winter, the alleys of Tourny in Bordeaux light up, transforming the city into a traditional Christmas market. Three main avenues structure this market:\n\n• The central aisle, the real heart of the market, where around a hundred chalets reveal a range of gift ideas, ranging from art objects to costume jewelry, including handcrafted toys.\n• The alley of gourmet terraces is a must for lovers of fine food and drinks. There, the scent of roasted chestnuts mixes with that of crispy waffles, melting tartiflette, and spicy mulled wine, without forgetting some Alsatian specialties for an exotic touch.\n• The alley of Artisans presents local talents: forty artisans from the Bordeaux region and elsewhere exhibit their creations, making this alley a real journey to discover French craftsmanship.\n\nBut the Bordeaux Christmas market is not just a gastronomic and artisanal experience. It is also a place of entertainment and festivity, especially for young people. Between musical shows, magic tricks, and creative workshops, the magic of Christmas operates at every moment.\n\n6. Nantes: Tradition and innovation at the Christmas market.\n\nAddress: Pl. Royale, 44000 Nantes, France\n\nFor decades Nantes has lit up and come alive around its must-see Christmas market, ranked among the most popular in France. On Place du Commerce and Place Royale, picturesque chalets stand, housing around a hundred passionate artisans.\n\nThese offer you a journey through authentic Christmas objects and decorations, as well as original gift ideas. And of course, culinary pleasures are not left out: roasted chestnuts, enveloping mulled wine, golden pancakes and soft waffles await.\n\nHowever, the city ​​of Nantes has always distinguished itself by combining tradition and innovation. Thus, alongside the traditional market, the city offers “L’autre Marché”.\n\nLocated on the Esplanade Carré Feydeau, this space highlights artisans and producers engaged in the social and solidarity economy. A place where each purchase takes on a special meaning, where each gift demonstrates an ethical and responsible approach. A great way to combine festivities and civic engagement.\n\n7. Lille: Wonder at the Christmas market.\n\nAddress: Pl. Rihour, 59800 Lille, France\n\nWith a tradition of over three decades, the Lille Christmas market continues to improve and expand. Every year, more exhibitors flock to present their original gift ideas and sparkling decorations that will make visitors’ eyes shine.\n\nThe excitement of the festivities comes to life on Place Rihour, brought to life by the regular appearances of Santa Claus, lively concerts, varied entertainment and magical illuminations that bathe the square.\n\nImpossible to talk about the Lille Christmas market without mentioning the majestic Ferris Wheel. It sits proudly and offers those who dare to climb it a breathtaking view of the rooftops of Lille. For adrenaline lovers, the caterpillar, positioned on the Place du Théâtre, promises good times of laughter and sensations.\n\nFamilies will find what they’re looking for with the little Christmas train that crisscrosses Place Richebé. Nearby, a gourmet stand will make young and old alike drool with an assortment of sweet treats. Undoubtedly, Lille is home to one of the most enchanting Christmas markets in France, combining magic, discovery and fun.\n\nAnd as if that wasn’t enough, the heart of Lille will sparkle with a thousand lights thanks to the Christmas lights. Dear visitors, a surprise awaits you at the market as soon as night begins to fall: our advice? Come and discover it for yourself!\n\n8. Marseille: Tradition and magic at the Christmas market.\n\nAddress: Old Port, Marseille, France\n\nThe Mediterranean sweetness of Marseille will certainly not see snow, but the festive and warm atmosphere of the Christmas markets takes over the streets every year. The Old Port comes alive with the traditional Christmas market, lined with chalets displaying a multitude of decorations, handicrafts, and tasty Provençal delicacies.\n\nCours Julien, for its part, is transformed into an effervescent artistic space. It is here that the Grand Marché de Noël des Créateurs comes to life, bringing together nearly 200 local talents. This is the perfect opportunity for those looking for unique gifts. Whether it’s painting, jewelry, lighting, photography, trendy accessories or designer furniture pieces, this market is a modern Ali Baba’s cave.\n\nBut it would be an omission not to mention the iconic Foire aux Santons, established on Place Charles de Gaulle. It is here that Provencal heritage is proudly displayed with the meticulous work of local santonniers, and craftsmen of these small figurines which populate Christmas nativity scenes. This meeting is an immersion in the ancestral traditions and folklore of Provence.\n\n9. Colmar: Dazzle at the winter Christmas market.\n\nAddress: Colmar, France\n\nNot only recognized as one of the most resplendent Christmas markets in France, the Christmas market of Colmar is often cited among the European jewels of this tradition. From the start of the festive season, Colmar takes on an enchanting atmosphere, with its multiple markets scattered along its picturesque streets:\n\n• The Place Jeanne d’Arc market, a true ode to Alsatian gastronomy, invites you to discover a myriad of regional products, from sweet treats to savory delights.\n• The Artisans Market, nestled in the heart of Koïfhus, is the theater of local artisanal excellence. We admire the expertise of potters, master glassmakers, wood carvers, ceramists and even talented hatters.\n• The Little Venice Market, with its magical atmosphere, is a true paradise for young people. The wooden houses are overflowing with enchanted toys, while a lively nativity scene, a sparkling merry-go-round and dazzling illuminations amaze young and old.\n\nIf you’re looking for authentic festive ornaments, the Place des Dominicains Christmas Market is your destination. The stalls are full of traditional and innovative decorations to beautify your celebrations.\n\nFinally, for epicureans in search of culinary delights, the last market is a gourmet invitation. Nine cabins welcome renowned chefs and master restaurateurs, ready to amaze you with their culinary creations. Whether it’s lunch, a convivial aperitif or a dinner under the stars, a real carnival of flavors awaits you.\n\n10. Lyon: Festival of Lights at the Christmas market.\n\nAddress: Place Carnot, Lyon, France\n\nIn Lyon, the end of the year is marked not only by the huge market that takes place at Place Carnot, but also by the famous Festival of Lights. If you can, plan your trip to take advantage of both events, because it’s worth the detour.\n\nThe works presented during the Festival of Lights are generally grandiose, they attract audiences from all over the world. As for the market, it brings together in a warm atmosphere nearly 90 artisan chalets who come to present their products. Activities are planned for children and various food stands will warm up those with a sweet tooth.\n\nTrue to its reputation, gastronomy is in the spotlight on the Lyon market. A culinary offering that can also be found on the city’s other market, that of Grand Hotel Dieu.\n\n11. Mulhouse: Unique textile magic at the Christmas market.\n\nAddress: Pl. de la Réunion, 68100 Mulhouse, France\n\nLooking for a unique Christmas market experience? Mulhouse should be at the top of your list. The particularity of this market lies in its remarkable tradition.\n\nIn fact, every year, the city unveils a new Christmas fabric, a decorative fabric specially created for the festivities. The chalets, the town hall and even the tram are adorned with this motif, giving the city an unparalleled charm.\n\nThe organization of the Mulhouse market revolves around Place de la Réunion, the beating heart of the festivities, where a period carousel majestically sits. A few steps away, the courtyard of the Temple Saint-Étienne hosts activities for children, including a Christmas bauble-making workshop.\n\nL’Étoile de Noël, located on the Place de la Réunion, is the rallying point where families and friends meet to share a mulled wine or a piece of kougelhopf. As for the Allée des Saveurs, it will seduce gourmets with its Alsatian specialties. Finally, don’t miss the Espace des Créateurs which highlights local talent by offering original artisanal creations.\n\nVisitors will also be dazzled by the immense illuminated tree. But also by the light show projected on the facade of the town hall, paying tribute to the rich textile history of the city.\n\nChristmas markets in France: FAQ.\n\nWhen do the Christmas markets start in Alsace?\n\nAlsace lights up and comes alive at the end of November, starting the Christmas Market season. For more information check out the calendar of Alsace Christmas markets.\n\nHow long do Christmas markets last?\n\nIn general, Christmas markets in France are held until Christmas Eve. However, some continue until December 31. For details, check local sites.\n\nLooking for more winter travel ideas? Check out the best winter vacations in the US and these magical European winter breaks,','kayak_682734ac19d80ca9fd6e1dc4abc50c4a.webp',1,'https://www.kayak.com/news/christmas-markets-in-france/','Nguồn: KAYAK - Lydia Rajteric','2026-09-18 17:08:43','2026-11-19 22:18:49'),(4,'The 10 best places to go on a cheap vacation with friends.','the-10-best-places-to-go-on-a-cheap-vacation-with-friends',2,'From beach breaks to city escapes, planning a cheap vacation with friends can seem like a financial challenge. But in my experience, going away as a group gives you more bang for your buck if you know where to go.\n\nHere are 10 of the best affordable group vacations, backed up by KAYAK flight data.\n\nAffordable group vacations backed by KAYAK data.\n\nCost top of mind? The below flights are based on average return costs and hotel prices are per night for a double room for two. Read on to get expert tips on how to find the best deals.\n\nBased on flight and hotel searches between Jan. 1, 2026 and Jun. 11, 2026 for travel between May 1, 2026 and Dec. 31, 2026 for 5+ travelers. Flight prices are based on round-trip, economy tickets; hotel rates are based on standard, double occupancy rooms. Prices are on average and are subject to change.\n\nChicago, Illinois\n\n• Best for: City strolling & architecture goggling\n• Group stay: Freehand Chicago\n\nThe Windy City is one of America’s most walkable cities. The Chicago Riverwalk puts you in the thick of the action. Start as a group at Lake Street and stroll its mile-plus tapestry of cafés and restaurants, bars and wineries, museums and street art. At its end, the incredible 18-mile Lakefront Trail opens up, hugging Lake Michigan’s shoreline and serving up skyline views you can’t buy.\n\nNashville, Tennessee\n\n• Best for: Live music & honky-tonk partying\n• Group stay: The Antioch Inn\n\nMusic City moves to its own beat, and it’s easy to get swept along for the ride. Travel the Honky Tonk Highway for year-round live music. Hop on the free Music City Circuit bus to take in the Ryman Auditorium, Country Music Hall of Fame and Lower Broadway where you can refuel at Robert’s Western World. Order the Recession Special – a fried Bologna sandwich, chips, beer and a moon pie for just $6.\n\nSan Diego, California\n\n• Best for: Beaches & surfing\n• Group stay: Avalon Buyout by AnantStay\n\nWith 70+ miles of public beach to laze on and world class waves to surf on, San Diego is the go-to for group adventure with an aquatic theme. The year-round perfect weather certainly helps. My pick is atmospheric La Jolla Cove for its evening sunsets and beach party picnics.\n\nLas Vegas, Nevada\n\n• Best for: Nightlife & celebrating\n• Group stay: Flamingo Las Vegas Hotel & Casino\n\nThe sheer spectacle and scale of the Strip is tailor made for groups with its cavernous casinos, incredible eateries and wild bars. To the north, neon-lit Fremont Street is easier on the budget with free nightly live shows, street performances and cheaper drinks. For an escape from the city’s madness, tour Red Rock Canyon’s scenic loop on the city outskirts.\n\nMiami, Florida\n\n• Best for: Culture & art\n• Group stay: Cottages El Portal\n\nMiami can be more affordable for group trips than people might think. Art and culture is everywhere, with regular free art tours and culture crawls across the city. In Little Havana, bar hop from free merengue lessons at Ball & Chain to cocktails at Bar Nancy. On the third Friday of every month, Calle Ocho comes to life with the Viernes Culturales, a block party not to be missed.\n\nNew York, New York\n\n• Best for: Iconic sights & experiences\n• Group stay: Moxy NYC Times Square\n\nFew cities in the world can match the city that never sleeps. Hit the streets of Manhattan for neck-craning views and world-famous landmarks. Take the free Staten Island Ferry to get up close to Lady Liberty.Time your visit with Broadway Week for two-for-one theater tickets or try the TKTS booth in Times Square (right by the Moxy Hotel) for half-price same-day tickets.\n\nSan Juan, Puerto Rico\n\n• Best for: A little bit of everything\n• Group stay: Fortaleza Suites Old San Juan\n\nMake it one to remember with a big trip to Puerto Rico – no passport needed for US citizens. The capital, San Juan, offers a bit of everything and caters to every taste, from the fine sands and glitzy towers of Condado Beach to the colorful and cultured Old Town.\n\nVancouver, British Columbia, Canada\n\n• Best for: Hiking & nature\n• Group stay: Luxury Home near Lonsdale Quay\n\nStanley Park is a rainforest within Vancouver’s city limits and epic for hiking. Rent bikes (tandems available!) and cycle the Stanley Park Seawall for rugged beaches and enormous views. Ride the Grouse Mountain Skyride aerial tram, big enough to carry groups to the city’s peak. Round out your stay with an organized craft brewery crawl through the many tasting rooms of Mount Pleasant.\n\nSeattle, Washington\n\n• Best for: Exploring by bike\n• Group stay: Green Tortoise Hostel\n\nSeattle loves cyclists. Use the budget-priced bike share program and hit the Urban Trail System to see the Emerald City’s neighborhoods, parks and waterfront at their best. Stop off at Pike’s Place Market for fish throwing, live music and a caffeine hit at the original Starbucks. Next door on Post Alley, add to America’s most disgusting artwork at the Gum Wall.\n\nNassau, The Bahamas\n\n• Best for: Doing as little as possible\n• Group stay: Breezes Bahamas\n\nNassau delivers the Caribbean at a pocket-friendly price. By day, glorious beaches, swimming with pigs and snorkeling the cavernous Thunderball Grotto. By night, Nassau and the fish fry at Arawak Cay complete with open-air live music, conch fritters and rum punches.\n\nExpert tips to get the best group flight and accommodation deals.\n\nKAYAK research shows that 68% of travelers are still planning to travel despite rising costs. Here’s how to make sure you’re getting the best deal for everyone.\n\n• Set a Price Alert. Takes the hassle and guesswork out of when to book your flight and accommodation. KAYAK’s Price Alerts monitor flight and hotel prices daily and alert you as they go up or down, so you don’t miss a deal.\n• Simplify with Ask AI. Skip the filters and plan your trip with KAYAK’s Ask AI tool. Ask conversational questions (“rental home in the Bahamas for eight people under $1,000 a night”) and get real-time results. Find direct flights and accommodation for everyone, plan detailed itineraries and share it all in the group chat. KAYAK data notes that 84% of travelers have had a group trip that’s never made it out of the group chat. Ask AI will change that.\n• Check the best time to travel. KAYAK’s Best Time to Travel tool uses your origin, destination and length of stay to tell you not only the best time to travel but also the best time to book your trip. When it comes to group bookings, this can save serious dollars.\n• Be flexible on travel dates. A little flexibility can go a long way toward easing the budget. Even just a day or two either side of your preferred travel dates can bring prices down. Traveling off season and mid week will also help. According to KAYAK data, January and November are the cheapest months for hotels, with Sunday check-ins tending to offer the best rates.\n• Be ready to move fast when you find a deal. I know from experience how hard it is wrangling a group to act quickly, book and pay for a deal when it pops up. Once you’ve agreed on a budget, gather everyone’s travel details and payments into one place. Then when that Price Alert with the right deal goes off, you’ll be ready to pounce.','kayak_67de6776efe13347cb198ff15100b733.webp',1,'https://www.kayak.com/news/places-to-go-on-vacation-with-friends/','Nguồn: KAYAK - Duncan Madden','2026-07-21 15:13:27','2026-11-19 22:02:07'),(5,'A guide on how to plan a group trip that will actually happen.','a-guide-on-how-to-plan-a-group-trip-that-will-actually-happen',3,'Some of my best travel experiences have been with others. Girlfriend getaways in California. A Jamaican road trip with three adventurous buddies. London with extended family.\n\nBut a great group trip takes effort to make happen. Here’s how to plan it well – whoever you’re traveling with and wherever you go.\n\nTips for planning a great group trip.\n\nEstablish the key components early.\n\nOnce you have a core group interested (about 3 or 4 people), you can start hashing out the most important elements of your trip:\n\n• Budget: Determine a price range everyone is willing to spend\n• Vibe: Is your crew craving a tropical beach getaway? A European city break with world-class museums and restaurants? A carefree road trip?\n• Dates and duration: Align on a time frame that works for everyone, taking into consideration that the longer you stay, the more it will cost\n\nAn easy way to get started is with KAYAK Explore. Explore lets you easily filter to find a destination based on the above – you can even search by great group trips.\n\nOnce you have some ideas in mind, get the ball rolling by sending a quick poll to your group via a messaging platform like WhatsApp, and make sure to specify the deadline to respond.\n\nOnce you have these general parameters established, the trip will start to take shape.\n\nAssign a trip leader, but have everyone pitch in.\n\nLuckily, people who love to travel tend to take on the role of trip leader. This task can include researching destinations, pricing options and making reservations.\n\nThat said, while your point person will oversee the big-picture stuff, everyone should contribute in some way (i.e., researching restaurant reviews, making reservations, etc.). This way, the main planner doesn’t feel overwhelmed, and it creates a sense of buy-in for everyone.\n\nBigger isn’t always better.\n\nGroup size plays a critical role in the overall trip experience. Generally speaking, the bigger the group, the more complicated the logistics, and the longer everything takes.\n\nFor example, expecting to snag a walk-in table for 10 people at a popular restaurant is far less likely than for a group of four. In my experience, the sweet spot is usually between four and six people.\n\nThat said, once the group has been established, I highly recommend keeping most conversations about the trip among yourselves (more on that below). That way, you avoid any potentially hurt feelings.\n\nCreate a dedicated group chat.\n\nGet the party started early with a dedicated group chat to build excitement and share key trip details. Here’s where you’ll alert everyone about key tasks like booking flights and stays while building anticipation.\n\nPlan for the last-minute holdouts.\n\nAccording to research from KAYAK, a whopping 90% of travelers agree that the biggest obstacle to making a group trip happen is those holdouts who won’t commit. To avoid this common dilemma, put these tips into your group trip toolbox:\n\n• Make sure deposits are paid early. This way, you’ll avoid last-minute changes – especially if they’re non-refundable.\n• Ask a couple of enthusiastic people to be vocal about their RSVPs early. This is what the group chat is made for. Seeing everyone else make plans and get excited can be the nudge the undecided need.\n\nPlan your trip early (but embrace impromptu getaways, too.)\n\nIn my experience, most group trips need at least four to six months’ advance planning – maybe even more if you’re headed abroad.\n\nThis has several advantages. First, it gives you plenty of time to take care of the logistics – like booking flights, hotels and creating an itinerary. Second, it allows for wiggle room for changes or more people who decide to join. And finally, you’ll have enough time to secure sought-after reservations – as well as better deals on flights and hotels.\n\nThat said, last-minute getaways can work if dates align. Especially if you spot a great deal.\n\nBuild an itinerary that includes group and free time.\n\nEveryone has a different travel style. Some like to charge full-force into a destination, while others (raises hand) prefer a more laid-back approach. But you can still build an itinerary that accommodates everyone and still feels like a shared experience. Here’s how:\n\n• Let people explore at their own pace. This way, the ambitious types can museum-hop to their hearts’ content, while the lounge-by-the-pool crowd can relax. A win-win!\n• Plan one group activity per day. In my experience, an evening meal is ideal. You can gather over dinner and trade stories about what everyone did during the day.\n• Don’t overschedule on travel days. Even for the most seasoned travelers in the bunch, trying to squeeze in too much on travel days or the day after a redeye flight is a recipe for disaster.\n\nLet your itinerary do the heavy lifting.\n\nKeep the group engaged and informed by creating an itinerary that handles the heavy lifting of real-time travel updates and enables easy collaboration – even offline. Here’s where KAYAK Trips comes in.\n\nTrips lets you plan, build and manage itineraries for trips of any length, in any location. Plus, it provides real-time updates on any cancellations, delays or gate changes.\n\nAnd shared editing permissions makes it a cinch for everyone to collaborate without the hassle of nonstop scrolling through the group chat or complicated spreadsheets.\n\nConsider a recurring trip date.\n\nOne way to eliminate the hassle and headache of aligning on dates? Pick a recurring date for your group trip like Labor Day weekend or the first week of October and let people adapt to it, instead of working around individual schedules.\n\nA standing date can make it easier to score deals too, since you know when it’s coming. Once you know the date, set a Price Alert and KAYAK will alert you when flight prices change.\n\nGo back to a destination you love.\n\nOnce you find a formula that works for your group, you don’t have to reinvent the wheel. A dear friend of mine and his extended family have been vacationing in St. Augustine every summer for decades. Their multi-family crew stays at the same mid-range resort every year, share cooking responsibilities, and split expenses like rental cars.\n\nThey’ve figured out a budget, destination and vibe that works for them – no decision fatigue or drama, just great memories. In fact, I crashed the party last year and had so much fun I’m bringing my son with me this summer.\n\nPractice makes perfect for group trips.\n\nWhile planning a group trip may seem daunting, the memories will be well worth it. And with some helpful strategies (like creating an itinerary that works for everyone), it’s easier than ever. You might even volunteer to plan your next group trip.','kayak_31f45579a08560278a5e92b557e64ced.webp',1,'https://www.kayak.com/news/how-to-plan-a-group-trip/','Nguồn: KAYAK - Blane Bachelor','2026-07-21 15:07:47','2026-11-19 22:02:09'),(6,'How to make a travel itinerary with KAYAK Trips.','how-to-make-a-travel-itinerary-with-kayak-trips',NULL,'Making a great travel itinerary doesn’t have to involve a bunch of apps, screenshots and travel panic. Our Trips tool keeps your itinerary, reservations and booking details in one place - no matter where you booked. Even offline, you can access essential details on the KAYAK app or your Apple Watch.\n\nAll you have to do is forward your bookings to trips@kayak.com. We’ll automatically create your travel itinerary from there. Traveling with friends or family? Add them so everyone can see the same details. Fewer “what time is check-in?” texts for you.\n\nCreate your itinerary with Trips.\n\nHere’s how to build a stress-free travel itinerary using Trips, without turning your camera roll into a confirmation-number graveyard.\n\nNote: for the best access to features, like offline use, download our App.\n\n1. Start with your transport.\n\nAdd your flights, car rental or other transport bookings first. If you’re traveling with a group, you can add your friends or family as collaborators.\n\nOnce added, you’ll get live updates on delays, cancellations and gate changes if they happen. Helpful when your gate moves from A4 to “somewhere in another zip code.”\n\nI love that you can find the booking number of the flight on the page. I always find that such a hassle when I’m at the airport or checking in. Fie Korum, Head of Content at KAYAK and avid Trips user\n\n2. Add your accommodation.\n\nNext up, where you’ll stay. Forward your booking or bookings, and Trips will automatically include check-in and check-out times, hotel addresses and confirmation numbers. So when you land tired, hungry and suddenly unsure where you’re going, your itinerary has your back.\n\n3. Plan your activities.\n\nNow for the fun part. Figure out what you and your travel companions want to do on your vacation. Book anything you think is likely to sell out, and forward those reservations to your Trip on KAYAK.\n\nThis could be tours, attractions or restaurant reservations. Just remember to leave some open space too. The best part of a trip is often the thing you didn’t schedule down to the minute.\n\n4. Share your itinerary.\n\nTrips are better together, after all. With Trips, you can easily share itineraries, collaborate on plans and keep fellow travelers in sync every step of the way. When we asked our VP of IT about his favorite Trips feature, this was top of the list:\n\nI love being able to share my trip itinerary with friends and family. All my trips are shared with my spouse and daughter by default. Tom Parker, VP of IT at KAYAK and avid Trips user\n\n5. Look back on all your vacations.\n\nAfter your vacation, indulge in some nostalgia. Trips keeps track of all your travel stats, from how many countries you visited to the airports you’ve traveled through and your top destinations. Feel like showing off? Simply hit share.\n\nMore Trips tips from KAYAK travel experts.\n\n• Use Trips to have your hotel address on hand, making it easier to find the first time.\n• If you have a longer or more complex vacation coming up, save all your details from various websites in Trips. From the exact time the train leaves for the airport, to all the tours/events you’ve booked.\n• Forget the name of that great hotel you stayed at? Check Trips to see all your old bookings.\n• Share the planning load with a friend by giving them collaborator access. This way you can both add bookings and keep track of the itinerary.\n\nFAQs\n\nWhat should a travel itinerary include?\n\nInclude your transport, accommodation, booking references, important times, addresses and planned activities. Keeping everything together makes it easier to find the info you need when you’re on the move.\n\nCan I create a Trips itinerary if I booked through different websites?\n\nYes. Our Trips tool keeps your travel bookings together in one itinerary, no matter where you booked.\n\nCan I access my itinerary without internet?\n\nYes. Trips lets you access your itinerary offline, even when you\'re in airplane mode or have limited connectivity.\n\nHow do I share my travel plans with someone?\n\nTrips allows you to share itineraries and collaborate with fellow travelers so everyone stays up to date. All you need to do is hit share and choose either view only or edit access.\n\nHow do I add bookings to Trips?\n\nSimply forward your booking confirmation emails to your dedicated Trips email address and our Trips tool will automatically organize them into your itinerary. Find more information on our Help Center.','kayak_31f45579a08560278a5e92b557e64ced.webp',1,'https://www.kayak.com/news/make-a-travel-itinerary/','Nguồn: KAYAK','2026-07-21 15:21:29','2026-11-19 22:02:11'),(7,'An easier way to get your trip right.','an-easier-way-to-get-your-trip-right',NULL,'Travel prices move fast. An AI answer is only as useful as the information behind it. Bringing you accurate, live travel information from hundreds of providers across flights, stays and rental cars is KAYAK’s expertise. Ask AI brings that expertise into the conversation, so you can explore ideas backed by fresh prices and real options.\n\nChat, use traditional search or combine both as your plans take shape. Live results and prices update alongside the conversation, so you can compare options, weigh the tradeoffs and keep planning without starting over.\n\nKAYAK is rolling out 28 new and enhanced AI-powered features to help you find travel dates that fit your budget, build personalized itineraries and get into the details before you book. Explore in your own words, then fine-tune with familiar filters and sorting tools. Here’s what’s new:\n\nStart with an idea, not a perfect search.\n\nAsk AI can help with open-ended plans, including finding a time to fly that fits your budget. It uses the technology behind KAYAK’s Buy vs. Wait recommendations to identify the cheapest dates, then checks them against live prices so you can see exactly what’s available and what it actually costs.\n\nYou can also turn one prompt into a personalized, day-by-day itinerary. Refine the plan through conversation while flight, hotel and rental car results update alongside it. And if you’re still weighing your options, set a Price Alert right in Ask AI to keep an eye on prices.\n\nAsk AI is now available in the KAYAK app on iOS and Android.\n\nCompare more than the fare.\n\nThe lowest price is only one part of choosing a flight. Ask AI can help you compare basic economy with main cabin, check baggage and cancellation policies, or find details about the aircraft for the specific flight and fare you’re viewing.\n\nWant Wi-Fi that can keep up with a streaming habit or an inbox? Use KAYAK’s new Starlink Wi-Fi filter or ask Ask AI to find flights with Starlink connectivity.\n\nRoute on-time performance information is coming soon, giving travelers another detail to weigh alongside price and schedule.\n\nFind a stay that fits the way you travel.\n\nA star rating can’t tell you everything about a hotel. Describe what matters instead, such as a rooftop pool or reviews that mention quiet rooms, and Ask AI will help find it even when there isn’t a standard filter for it.\n\nAI-powered summaries under each hotel, like “kid-friendly swimming pools,” can also help you get a feel for the place before you book. And coming soon, Ask AI will be able to remember preferences you’ve shared, like nonstop flights or morning departures, to help personalize future recommendations.\n\nA more useful way to plan.\n\nAI can make it easier to explore what’s possible. But the right trip is still your call. Ask AI brings your conversation, live results and the details that matter into one place, so you can compare, refine and decide what works for you.\n\nOpen KAYAK and start with a question. You don’t need to have it all figured out yet.','kayak_c84a060e6f197a8e2d8b0229f385b6dd.webp',1,'https://www.kayak.com/news/ask-ai-updates/','Nguồn: KAYAK','2026-11-17 19:00:00','2026-11-19 22:02:13'),(8,'KAYAK’s monthly flight deal tracker.','kayak-s-monthly-flight-deal-tracker',NULL,'Looking for a trip but not ready to sell your first-born? Look no further. We’ve analyzed millions of searches on KAYAK to find you the lowest average round-trip economy fares (say that 3 times fast) from your nearest airports.\n\nWe’re talking round-trip flights under $200 within the US and up to $600 internationally. All travel is for next month, so you have time to plan. So see where the value is and get your trips right this season.\n\nBy the numbers.\n\nCheapest domestic route\n\nAtlanta to ???????? Jacksonville, FL: $65.\n\nCheapest international route\n\nPhoenix to ???????? Vancouver: $184.\n\nLargest month-over-month price drop\n\nBoston to ???????? Des Moines, IA down 69%.\n\nAverage airfares in November 2026, round-trip, economy class.\n\nPick your region.\n\n1. Cheap flights from the Northeast\n\n2. Cheap flights from the Midwest\n\n3. Cheap flights from the South\n\n4. Cheap flights from the West\n\nCheap flights from Northeastern US.\n\nThis month’s stand out deal: from Philadelphia to ???????? Castries, Saint Lucia, $597, down 20% compared to last month.\n\nCheck the precise flight prices for your destination here.\n\nCheap flight from Midwestern US.\n\nThis month’s stand out deal: from Detroit to ???????? Oranjestad, Aruba, $489, down 32% compared to last month.\n\nCheck the precise flight prices for your destination here.\n\nCheap flights from Southern US.\n\nThis month’s stand out deal: from Charlotte to ???????? Providenciales, Turks and Caicos Islands, $510, down 38% compared to last month.\n\nCheck the precise flight prices for your destination here.\n\nCheap flights from Western US.\n\nThis month’s stand out deal: from Denver to ???????? Brussels, Belgium, $514, down 38% compared to last month.\n\nCheck the precise flight prices for your destination here.\n\nMethodology\n\nMonthly flight data\n\nThe presented insights are based on flight searches made on KAYAK in the last 90 days accessed on the 20th of each month for traveling in the upcoming month from the defined US airports. Flight prices were compared with those for travel to the same destinations in the previous and following months, based on searches during the same search period. Searches within 10 days of departure are not considered for the analysis.\n\nKAYAK’s cheapest routes are based on the averages of our cheapest (featured as cheapest on KAYAK) round-trip flight prices on economy class. Domestic round-trip fares are capped at $200, international round-trip fares are capped at $600.\n\nPrices may vary, percentages for changes in prices are approximate. Route exclusions may have been applied in cases of insufficient data or according to the official travel advisory at the time when the data analysis is conducted.','kayak_04a97124771354ffc90e4f60925e0313.webp',1,'https://www.kayak.com/news/monthly-flight-deals/','Nguồn: KAYAK','2026-10-26 15:09:11','2026-11-19 22:02:15'),(9,'The best time to book a flight & the cheapest days to fly.','the-best-time-to-book-a-flight-the-cheapest-days-to-fly',4,'Several factors affect the cost of airfare — destination, demand, time of year — and they often change. Understanding these indicators is key to booking flights at the cheapest time and flying on the cheapest days.\n\nTo help you find the best days to book (and travel), I analyzed KAYAK’s data on one-way and round-trip flights, both domestic and international. The findings reveal that while there isn’t a single best day to book, flying on certain days can save you money.\n\nIn this article.\n\nThe best day to book flights (domestic and international)When to fly domestically?When to travel internationally?Tools to help you find the best time to bookBest time to book FAQs\n\nQuick summary.\n\n• Best time to book domestic flights? Around 30 days in advance for domestic\n• Best time to book international flights? Around 1 – 2 weeks in advance\n• Cheapest days to fly within the US: Depart Monday, return Wednesday\n• Cheapest days to fly internationally: Depart Wednesday, return Wednesday\n\nWhen is the best time to book a flight?\n\nWhile the data on what day to book isn’t concrete, it does suggest a few trends for the best time to book flights. But instead of a particular day of the week, it’s about how far in advance you book.\n\nBest time to book a domestic flight.\n\nFor domestic flights, buy flights around 30 days ahead of departure to score the lowest average price, at $228.\n\nBest time to book international flights.\n\nThe best time to book international flights, meanwhile, turned up a bit of a surprise. According to the data, the best time to book an international flight is 1 – 2 weeks ahead of time, at an average cost of $666. Proceed with caution!\n\nGood news for when wanderlust strikes at the last minute.\n\nWhat about during busy travel periods?\n\nPopular travel periods tend to mean higher fares. Book flights early when you can for:\n\n• Summer vacation (June-August)\n• Thanksgiving week (especially Wednesday before and Sunday after)\n• Christmas through New Year’s\n• Spring break\n• Three-day holiday weekends (Memorial Day, Labor Day, etc.)\n\nWhat are the cheapest days to fly domestic?\n\n• Cheapest day to fly out: Monday (~$265)\n• Cheapest day to return: Wednesday (~$194)\n\nOverall, you’ll get slightly cheaper outbound fares by flying early in the week, but the real win is returning midweek, where savings are more considerable. One-way flights are also cheaper midweek. Avoid Friday or Sunday returns, when prices tend to be more expensive.\n\nWhat are the cheapest days to fly international?\n\n• Cheapest day to fly out: Wednesday (~$747)\n• Cheapest day to return: Wednesday (~$604)\n\nFor International travel, Wednesday offers the best deals. Apart from one-way flights, which are cheapest by a slight margin on Mondays.\n\nLike domestic travel, midweek flying is, on average, slightly more cost-effective than weekend flying. As you might expect, Friday–Saturday flights are the most expensive (especially return flights).\n\nWhat’s the cheapest day to buy flights?\n\nInsights from Kayla DeLoache, travel trends expert at KAYAK.\n\nThere’s no magic day to book a flight. There are several reasons why, but here are the two biggest:\n\n• Seasonality: If you search for a last-minute summer vacation on a Tuesday, it’s unlikely you’ll find a cheap flight. If you search for a trip that’s three months away during the off-season, you might. What matters most is demand, not the day you search.\n• How quickly seats are sold: Similarly, if a flight fills up quickly, it won’t matter what day you book. Popular flights that are nearly full tend to be more expensive, while less popular flights with plenty of empty seats are often cheaper.\n\nIf you’re looking for cheap flights, focus on how far in advance you book and the time of day you fly. Very early morning or very late departures, such as red-eye flights, can be cheaper. Having some flexibility with your travel dates can help, too.\n\nWhat else to know about the best dates to book travel?\n\nAirfares fluctuate for all kinds of reasons, and KAYAK’s data is merely a reflection of overall trends. That said, no matter when you fly, KAYAK can help you fin the best deals. As an award winning metsearch engine, KAYAK does the work for you.\n\nSo even if your travel plans don’t always allow you to book on days with the lowest airfares, KAYAK can help you find a airfare that’s affordable for you, whether you’re looking to explore the U.S. or see the rest of the world.\n\nMonthly best priced flight routes from your home airport in March.\n\nKAYAK tools to help you find the cheapest time to book and fly.\n\nKAYAK offers a variety of Flight Filters to customize your search, including a price filter to keep your budget in line. That’s not enough to determine the cheapest time to buy flights, though, so there are plenty more free tools to help you get there.\n\n• KAYAK Price Alerts: Know when prices go up or down with Price Alerts. Set alerts for specific routes and receive email or phone notifications when prices drop. Run a flight search and try it now.\n• KAYAK Price Forecast: Find this tool when you run a flight search on KAYAK. Price Forecast analyzes historical data to predict whether prices on your route are likely to rise or fall, helping you decide whether to book now or wait.\n• Flexible date search: Use KAYAK’s flight search calendar to compare prices ±3 days from your selected travel dates, full weekends, or entire months. Green indicates cheaper.\n• KAYAK Explore: Don’t mind where you travel to? See which destinations are cheapest to fly to from your home airport using KAYAK Explore.\n\nFor best results, set any alerts 2-4 months before your intended travel dates. When you see a price that meets your budget, book it – waiting for a lower fare that may never come can backfire.\n\nBest time to book flights: FAQ\n\nHow do airlines price tickets?\n\nEvery airline’s goal is to get the most money possible for each seat sold, which means airfare pricing is one of the purest examples of supply and demand in action. To do this, popular leisure routes and economy cabins are priced high further in advance, which is when vacation travelers tend to book.\n\nThese seats tend to go down over time as the airline tries to fill the flight before departure. Business routes and premium cabins (business/first) are priced lower upfront to fill minimum quotas, then hiked over time as corporate travelers, who tend to book closer to their trips, scramble to get a remaining seat.\n\nDo flight prices go down on Tuesday?\n\nThe oft-repeated theory that it’s cheaper to purchase airfare on Tuesdays is simply not true. We analyzed airline price trends every day of the week and busted this myth with the discovery that observing demand trends over time was far more important than booking on a specific day of the week.\n\nWhat day of the week are flights cheapest?\n\nThe cheapest days to fly are the days when fewer other travelers are flying. Friday and Sunday are heavily traveled for weekend events and getaways, but Tuesday and Wednesday are the least traveled and tend to be the cheapest.\n\nHowever, our latest data turned up some interesting trends regarding international airfare. We found that the cheapest time to book a flight outside the U.S. was about a week in advance, so keep an eye out for last-minute deals (or set a Price Alert).\n\nDo flights get cheaper closer to the departure date?\n\nThis is more common in economy class cabins and on leisure travel routes (i.e., vacation destinations). For business class and first class seats, or routes are frequented by corporate travelers, the reverse is more often true, with prices increasing as the departure approaches.\n\nHow often do flight prices change?\n\nThe short answer here can be frustratingly unhelpful: Flight prices change as often as airlines see fit. As the flight fills up (or doesn’t), airlines will continuously change prices to maximize their potential profit, which means fares may not only change daily but can even multiple times throughout the day. Flight prices vary as well depending on the season, the seats you want to book and ultimately the balance between supply and demand of seats.\n\nDo flight prices go down at night?\n\nSometimes. Airfare prices change regularly based on supply and demand, and the airline’s resulting prediction of how it can maximize income on remaining seats. Computer software is often in charge of analyzing this and updating fares. This can certainly happen at night, but it is equally like to happen any time of day, any day of the week.\n\nAbout this guide.\n\nThis article is based on KAYAK search data for 2026. To provide you with the best tips on when to fly, we analyzed this data for both international and domestic travel. This blog was originally published on June 26, 2023, and updated on August 26, 2024. Blane Bachelor refreshed the data on July 28, 2025. KAYAK’s team of editors updated all data points again for 2026 on March 26, 2026.\n\nMethodology\n\nAverage flight prices of economy class per day of the week and for each 7 – 183 search days ahead of departure in 2026 for round-trips broken down by departures and returns aggregated for domestic and international routes, respectively. KAYAK analyzed search data conducted on kayak.com between 01/01/2025 – 18/03/2026 for traveling anytime until 30/06/2026','kayak_89721f58414f872e41d512b138ebf494.webp',1,'https://www.kayak.com/news/when-are-flights-cheapest/','Nguồn: KAYAK - Brandon Schultz','2026-04-01 19:41:49','2026-11-19 22:02:18');
